quences. [truncated]","original_chars":142712},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Problem Formulation and Method Overview","heading_level":3,"line_start":56,"line_end":63,"text":"Fig. 2 summarizes our pipeline. We recast DNA sequence modeling as a document understanding problem. Given a genomic sequence S = ( s 1 , . . . , s N ) of length N , where each s i ∈ { A,C,G,T,N } , we render it into a structured multi-page DNA document D ( S ) consisting of P pages:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere p indexes the pages, page p ∈ R H × W × 3 , and B ( p ) denotes bounding boxes of DNA intervals on page p (see Section 3.2). To support coordinate-indexed reasoning, we define an interval-to-region mapping Φ : ( i, j ) ↦→ b ij and inverse region-to-interval mapping Φ -1 . Each region is represented as a bounding box b ij = [ img id, x 1 , y 1 , x 2 , y 2 ] , with img id ∈ { 0 , . . . , P -1 } indicating the page index and ( x 1 , y 1 , x 2 , y 2 ) specified in the original page pixel coordinates. 0 ≤ i &lt; j &lt; N defines a contiguous interval in S ; b ij is extracted from the dense annotations {B ( p ) } in DNA document D . Based on Φ and {B ( p ) } , we instantiate prompts q ∈ Q for six OCR-style tasks spanning four genomic primitives (recognition, grounding, retrieval, completion; see Section 3.3). To understand D , we pretrain a visual encoder E θ and a document decoder G ψ with prompt-conditioned document supervision: the encoder takes only the page images and produces visual tokens Z = E θ ( { page p } ) , and the decoder generates task outputs ˆ Y = G ψ ( Z, q ) (see Section 3.4). This shifts learning from global sequence-token prediction to prompt-driven region supervision over a spatially indexed genome. [truncated]","original_chars":1833},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Representation Consistency under Formatting Perturbations","heading_level":5,"line_start":1368,"line_end":1379,"text":"We further evaluate whether identical DNA 